      "text": "To find the ratio of the volumes of the two cubes, we need to calculate the volume of each cube and then divide the volume of the larger cube by the volume of the smaller cube.\n\nFirst, let's recall the formula for the volume of a cube. If the edge length of a cube is $s$, then the volume $V$ of the cube is given by:\n\n\\[V = s^3\\]\n\nFor the smaller cube with an edge length of six inches, we have:\n\n\\[V_1 = 6^3 = 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 216\\]\n\nFor the larger cube with an edge length of one foot, we first need to convert this length to inches (since the volume of the smaller cube is in inches):\n\n\\[1\\text{ foot} = 12\\text{ inches}\\]\n\nNow, the volume of the larger cube is:\n\n\\[V_2 = 12^3 = 12 \\times 12 \\times 12 = 1728\\]\n\nNow, we can find the ratio of the volume of the larger cube to the volume of the smaller cube:\n\n\\[\\text{Ratio} = \\frac{V_2}{V_1} = \\frac{1728}{216}\\]\n\nTo simplify this fraction, we find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 1728 and 216. Using the Euclidean algorithm, we find that the GCD is 216:\n\n\\[\\frac{1728 \\div 216}{216 \\div 216} = \\frac{8}{1}\\]\n\nThus, the simplified fraction representing the ratio of the volume of the cube with an edge length of six inches to the volume of the cube with an edge length of one foot is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{\\frac{8}{1}}\\]",
